/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Processing Processing
 * @ingroup DspCore
 * @brief Typed, zero-copy fixed-size sample-block views.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Processing_Model Ownership and processing model
 * DspCore separates signal memory from signal processing:
 *
 * @code
 * Caller-owned input samples ---> SampleBlock<const T> ---> DspCore algorithm
 * Caller-owned output samples <--- SampleBlock<T>       <--- DspCore algorithm
 * @endcode
 *
 * SampleBlock is a small, non-owning view over externally owned contiguous
 * samples. It never allocates, copies, frees, queues, resamples, schedules,
 * timestamps, or routes samples. The caller owns the backing memory and keeps
 * it alive for the duration of the Process call.
 *
 * DspCore algorithms own no external input or output block. They may own
 * internal algorithm state: FIR owns taps and delay history, while IIR owns
 * numerator, denominator, and delay histories. This makes a JUCE callback or
 * a microcontroller DMA buffer usable without an intermediate copy.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Processing_Typed Typed n-to-n blocks
 * The owner normally constructs the input and output views when it prepares
 * its buffers, then can reuse those views across process calls:
 * @code{.cpp}
 * const float audio[] = { 1.0F, 0.0F };
 * float result[2]{};
 *
 * const SampleBlock<const float> audioBlock{audio, 2U};
 * const SampleBlock<float> resultBlock{result, 2U};
 *
 * Filters::FIR<float> filter;
 * filter.Configure(taps, tapCount);
 * filter.Process(audioBlock, resultBlock);
 * @endcode
 * `MakeInputBlock(data, size)` and `MakeOutputBlock(data, size)` provide the
 * same views at an integration boundary that only exposes a raw pointer and
 * size, such as an audio callback. Neither form copies the samples.
 *
 * `SampleBlock<const float>` makes the input read-only at compile time, while
 * `SampleBlock<float>` represents writable output. SampleBlock itself may
 * describe any element type; a particular DspCore algorithm can impose a
 * narrower requirement. FIR and IIR require arithmetic SampleType values.
 *
 * The block extent is its `size`: there is deliberately no capacity or
 * produced-count distinction in this layer. FIR and IIR are synchronous n-to-n
 * transforms and reject invalid blocks or unequal input/output sizes before
 * touching their state or output. Empty blocks are valid and retain state.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Processing_Access Lifetime and aliasing
 * A block is a borrowed view, not a container. It must not outlive the caller's
 * memory. A filter may process exactly in place when input and output start at
 * the same address. Partially overlapping, different ranges are not supported.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Processing_WhatThisIsNot What processing does not provide
 * DspCore is not a graph owner, stream clock, sample-rate converter, thread
 * synchronizer, FIFO, MIDI/event transport, or port-routing system. A future
 * stream/runtime library will own those concerns, including heterogeneous
 * audio, MIDI, envelope and event transport. It can adapt its buffers to these
 * typed blocks at the boundary.
 */

/**
 * @namespace DspCore::Processing
 * @ingroup DspCore_Processing
 * @brief Non-owning fixed-size sample views used by DspCore algorithms.
 */
